## Phase 2 — Intent Intelligence
*Make the intent pipeline robust enough to handle real users who don't know how to write good prompts.*

### 2.1 — Vague Intent Detection + Clarification Flow (MODIFY `intentClassifier.ts`)

**Problem solved:** Users typing "make it smart" or "chatbot" get meaningless validation results.

**Vagueness detector — deterministic, free:**

Before any embedding or LLM call, run a simple heuristic check on the raw intent string:

```typescript
function isIntentVague(intent: string): { vague: boolean; reason: string } {
  const trimmed = intent.trim();

  // Too short
  if (trimmed.split(" ").length < 4)
    return { vague: true, reason: "too_short" };

  // Generic single-word category (no domain specifics)
  const genericTerms = ["chatbot", "assistant", "ai", "smart", "helpful",
                        "automation", "tool", "bot", "system"];
  const words = trimmed.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
  const isAllGeneric = words.every(w => genericTerms.includes(w));
  if (isAllGeneric)
    return { vague: true, reason: "all_generic" };

  // No noun phrase (very rough heuristic — no word longer than 4 chars)
  const hasSubstantiveWord = words.some(w => w.length > 4 && !genericTerms.includes(w));
  if (!hasSubstantiveWord)
    return { vague: true, reason: "no_domain" };

  return { vague: false, reason: "" };
}
```

**UI response to vague intent:**

Don't block the user or show an error. Instead, show a suggestion panel beneath the intent field:

```
💡 Your intent is quite general. A more specific description
   gives you more accurate results.

   Instead of: "customer service chatbot"
   Try:        "a chatbot that handles product return requests
                and shipping queries for an e-commerce store"

   [ Use suggestion ] [ Keep mine ]
```

The "use suggestion" button triggers **Intent Expansion** (2.2 below). "Keep mine" proceeds with the original.

**This is triggered before any pipeline stage runs** — the user sees this before clicking "Run Validation."

