### 2.3 — Document Type Detection (NEW: add to `pdfLoader.ts`)

**Problem solved:** Code documentation, statistical research papers, and legal contracts get incorrectly penalized by a quality rubric calibrated for narrative prose.

**What to add:**

After text extraction, classify the document's content type using a simple heuristic (no LLM needed):

```typescript
type DocumentType = "prose" | "technical-code" | "statistical" | "legal" | "mixed";

function detectDocumentType(extractedText: string): DocumentType {
  const text = extractedText.toLowerCase();
  const codeSignals = (text.match(/```|function |def |const |import |class |return |=>|{|}/g) || []).length;
  const statSignals = (text.match(/p\s*[<>]\s*0\.\d|mean|std|n\s*=\s*\d|figure \d|table \d|\bp-value\b/g) || []).length;
  const legalSignals = (text.match(/whereas|hereinafter|notwithstanding|pursuant|indemnif/g) || []).length;
  const total = Math.max(codeSignals + statSignals + legalSignals, 1);

  if (codeSignals / total > 0.5) return "technical-code";
  if (statSignals / total > 0.4) return "statistical";
  if (legalSignals / total > 0.4) return "legal";
  if (codeSignals + statSignals + legalSignals > 20) return "mixed";
  return "prose";
}
```

**How document type changes downstream behaviour:**

| Type | Quality scoring prompt adjustment | Intent check adjustment |
|---|---|---|
| `prose` | Standard rubric | Standard |
| `technical-code` | Rubric changes: values structure, completeness, example coverage. Ignores "readability" as prose | Similarity threshold lowered to 0.35 (technical terms are sparse in embeddings) |
| `statistical` | Values data completeness, defined variables, methodology. Ignores "grounding in plain English" | Standard |
| `legal` | Values clause completeness, defined terms, numbered structure | Standard |
| `mixed` | Run both rubrics, take the max score | Lowered threshold |

Pass `documentType` to `scoreChunkQuality()` and `checkIntentMatch()`.

